WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.720
How fast does a nerve signal race through a limb?

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.040
And how can you read active muscle fibres under the skin?

3
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.020
Two linked methods answer both.

4
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.620
Nerve conduction velocity times axon impulses, while electromyography, or EMG,

5
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.100
records muscle spikes.

6
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.600
An action potential is a brief, self-renewing electrical impulse.

7
00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:33.140
Response time adds each axon's length divided by its speed, plus delays at the ends.

8
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.880
From one point, those end delays blur speed.

9
00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:42.920
Stimulate at two points, subtract arrival times, and shared end delays cancel.

10
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:49.040
Dividing distance between those points by time difference gives conduction velocity,

11
00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:50.760
the impulse's speed.

12
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.900
Speed rises with axon thickness and myelin, tight layers of glial membrane.

13
00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.220
Short bare gaps, the nodes of Ranvier, pack sodium channels densely.

14
00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:08.360
The impulse is renewed at each node, so it seems to jump: saltatory conduction.

15
00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:15.073
Test the math on a 1.00 m path from foot to spinal cord, counting conduction alone.

16
00:01:16.553 --> 00:01:19.273
Travel time equals distance divided by speed.

17
00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:25.293
An unmyelinated C fibre can conduct at 0.5 m/s.

18
00:01:26.593 --> 00:01:28.893
How long does its impulse take to reach the cord?

19
00:01:34.417 --> 00:01:39.317
1.00 m divided by 0.5 m/s gives 2 seconds.

20
00:01:40.477 --> 00:01:44.917
At 2 m/s, a C fibre takes 0.5 seconds.

21
00:01:46.257 --> 00:01:51.417
Thinly myelinated Aδ fibres run roughly 5 to 30 m/s.

22
00:01:52.817 --> 00:01:58.677
Large myelinated axons at about 100 m/s take one hundredth of a second.

23
00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.750
Now follow a motor axon to skeletal muscle.

24
00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:09.090
At the neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine starts an action potential along each

25
00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:14.430
fibre. One alpha motor neuron and its fibres form a motor unit.

26
00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:20.730
Fibres are not electrically connected, so each fires only when its own motor neuron

27
00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:27.010
fires. EMG records those muscle spikes, revealing active motor units.

28
00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:30.273
Three guardrails keep records honest.

29
00:02:31.573 --> 00:02:36.873
Impulses are all-or-none, so stronger force adds firing rate and motor units, not

30
00:02:36.873 --> 00:02:43.513
taller spikes. A teaching model assumes about 1 ms per cord synapse, yet real

31
00:02:43.513 --> 00:02:46.713
synapses vary, so delay cannot prove the count.

32
00:02:48.033 --> 00:02:53.293
And some C fibres carry touch, not pain, so speed alone cannot fix what a fibre

33
00:02:53.293 --> 00:02:54.293
reports.

34
00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:00.800
In 1850, Helmholtz timed a frog nerve at two points: a few tens of metres per

35
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:07.320
second. Later timing showed that thin bare fibres manage about 1 metre per second or

36
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:13.820
less. The largest myelinated mammalian fibres reach about 100, or a little more.

37
00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:21.090
On an EMG trace, graded force shows as more motor units firing, and firing more

38
00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:27.950
often. Time impulses across distance, and listen to muscle spikes, to test nerves

39
00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:29.210
and motor units together.
